[?25l[0m[38;5;141m> [0mThe translation is in [38;5;10mtranslation/[0m ([38;5;10mCargo.toml[0m, [38;5;10msrc/main.rs[0m) and builds clean with [38;5;10mcargo build --release[0m. All 26 differential tests against the compiled C original produced byte-identical stdout.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The program is small: read one integer with [38;5;10mscanf("%d")[0m, build a [38;5;10mhouse_t {floors=x, bedrooms=3, bathrooms=2.0}[0m, [38;5;10mmemcpy[0m it into a [38;5;10mchar[][0m, and print the raw object bytes as lowercase hex followed by a newline.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Two things drove most of the work:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Struct object representation. [38;5;10mhouse_t[0m is [38;5;10m{int, int, double}[0m — 16 bytes on the LP64 little-endian target, fields at offsets 0/4/8, no padding holes. Since the C uses [38;5;10m= {0}[0m before assigning fields, every byte is defined, so I reproduce the layout explicitly by writing each field's [38;5;10mto_le_bytes()[0m into a zeroed [38;5;10m[u8; 16][0m rather than transmuting. Output is always [38;5;10m<floors LE>[0m + [38;5;10m03000000[0m + [38;5;10m0000000000000040[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mscanf("%d")[0m fidelity. I wrote a byte-at-a-time stdin reader so parsing consumes exactly what C consumes and skips whitespace across newlines. Two behaviors were easy to get wrong:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- On matching failure or EOF (empty input, [38;5;10mabc[0m, a lone [38;5;10m-[0m), [38;5;10mscanf[0m returns without writing, so [38;5;10mx[0m keeps its initial [38;5;10m0[0m. The Rust returns [38;5;10mNone[0m and leaves [38;5;10mx = 0[0m.[0m[0m
- Out-of-range input is UB in C, but glibc has concrete behavior: the value saturates to [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m/[38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m as a 64-bit [38;5;10mlong[0m, then narrows to [38;5;10mint[0m. So [38;5;10m99999999999999999999999999[0m yields [38;5;10mffffffff[0m (i.e. [38;5;10m-1[0m), not a clamp to [38;5;10mINT_MAX[0m. I reproduce the saturate-then-truncate path; the tests confirm it for both signs and around the [38;5;10mi64[0m/[38;5;10mi32[0m boundaries.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Worth noting: this matches glibc on x86-64 Linux. The overflow behavior is a libc implementation detail rather than something the C standard guarantees, so a different libc could differ on those inputs — the in-range cases are portable.[0m[0m
